	"nodes":[
		{"id":"894ecdc0644bf72e","type":"text","text":"# **New Homework** \n### - **Study Grammar 4 and Grammar 5 and finish the remaining E exercises.**","x":-325,"y":62,"width":650,"height":172,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"5b1c8d9aa4e9ab65","type":"text","text":"\n## **13:35** -\tIntroduction to 30 Seconds \n## **13:50** -\t 30 Seconds Game\n## **14:20** -   Homework for next week\n\n","x":-324,"y":-133,"width":649,"height":156,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"de2d86d4382775e0","type":"text","text":"# **Today's Class: Writing and Grammar**","x":-301,"y":-234,"width":602,"height":68,"color":"2"},
		{"id":"a6e1dbd5777214d0","x":492,"y":-200,"width":793,"height":757,"color":"3","type":"text","text":"# THE PAST PERFECT\n\n## 1. What is the Past Perfect?\n\nWe use the Past Perfect to talk about an action that happened before another action or moment in the past.\n\nWhen we talk about two things that happened in the past, the Past Perfect helps us understand which action happened first.\n\nExample:\n\nI had finished my homework before my mum came home.\n\nTwo things happened:\n\n- First: I finished my homework.\n    \n- Second: My mum came home.\n    \n\nThe first action uses the Past Perfect:\n\nhad finished\n\nThe second action uses the Past Simple:\n\ncame"},
		{"id":"41f47054a6649ba9","x":1559,"y":645,"width":440,"height":1207,"color":"3","type":"text","text":"## How do we form the Past Perfect?\n\nGRAMMAR FORMULA\n\n## Subject + had + past participle \n\nWe use had with every subject.\n\n|Subject|Had|Past participle|\n|---|---|---|\n|I|had|eaten|\n|You|had|finished|\n|He / She / It|had|gone|\n|We|had|studied|\n|They|had|arrived|\n\n### Positive sentences (+)\n\n- I had eaten breakfast before school.\n    \n- She had finished her test before the bell rang.\n    \n- They had left when we arrived.\n    \n- We had studied English before the exam.\n    \n\n### Negative sentences (-)\n\nSubject + had not + past participle\n\nWe can shorten had not to hadn't.\n\n- I hadn't finished my homework.\n    \n- She hadn't eaten breakfast before school.\n    \n- They hadn't seen the film before.\n    \n- We hadn't studied enough before the exam.\n    \n\n### Questions (?)\n\nHad + subject + past participle?\n\n- Had you finished your homework?\n    \n- Had she already left?\n    \n- Had they eaten before the party?\n    \n- Had he studied for the test?"},
		{"id":"296f1c6042a9cb9b","x":1376,"y":-200,"width":616,"height":412,"color":"3","type":"text","text":"### Compare\n\nPast Simple:\n\n\"I finished my homework at 6 o'clock.\"\n\nThis tells us about one completed action in the past.\n\nPast Perfect:\n\n\"I had finished my homework before my friend arrived.\"\n\nThis tells us that the homework was already finished when my friend arrived."},
		{"id":"5b90763dea1b4424","x":749,"y":809,"width":471,"height":952,"color":"3","type":"text","text":"## What is a past participle?\n\nA past participle is the third form of a verb, also called V3.\n\nRegular verbs usually end in -ed.\n\n|Base verb (V1)|Past Simple (V2)|Past participle (V3)|\n|---|---|---|\n|work|worked|worked|\n|play|played|played|\n|finish|finished|finished|\n|study|studied|studied|\n|clean|cleaned|cleaned|\n|eat|ate|eaten|\n|go|went|gone|\n|see|saw|seen|\n|write|wrote|written|\n|do|did|done|\n|take|took|taken|\n|forget|forgot|forgotten|\n|leave|left|left|\n|buy|bought|bought|\n\nImportant: Always use the past participle after had.\n\nIncorrect: She had went home.\n\nCorrect: She had gone home.\n\nIncorrect: They had ate dinner.\n\nCorrect: They had eaten dinner."},
		{"id":"87cf5077457c531b","x":215,"y":809,"width":488,"height":609,"color":"3","type":"text","text":"### Use had + past participle.\n\n1. She __________ (finish) her homework before dinner.\n    \n2. They __________ (leave) when we arrived.\n    \n3. I __________ (eat) breakfast before I went to school.\n    \n4. He __________ (forget) his password.\n    \n5. We __________ (see) that film before.\n    \n6. My brother __________ (break) his phone.\n    \n7. The teacher __________ (start) the lesson before I arrived.\n    \n8. Sarah __________ (write) the email before lunchtime."},
		{"id":"765278f548069f57","x":215,"y":1488,"width":412,"height":333,"color":"3","type":"text","text":"## Make the sentences negative\n\nExample:\n\nI had finished my work.\n\nI hadn't finished my work.\n\n1. She had eaten lunch.\n    \n2. They had studied for the exam.\n    \n3. He had cleaned his room.\n    \n4. We had seen the film.\n    \n5. Tom had done his homework."},
		{"id":"3d70642b51a89100","x":-235,"y":1931,"width":560,"height":1408,"color":"3","type":"text","text":"# PREPOSITIONS OF TIME\n\n## 1. What are prepositions?\n\nPrepositions are words that tell us when something happens or where something is.\n\nFor time, we often use:\n\nIN, ON and AT\n\nOther examples include:\n\nBEFORE, AFTER, DURING, FOR, SINCE, UNTIL and BY.\n\n## 2. Preposition of time: IN\n\nWe use IN for longer periods of time.\n\n## IN = months, years, seasons, parts of the day\n\n|Use|Example|\n|---|---|\n|Months|in January|\n|Years|in 2026|\n|Seasons|in summer|\n|Centuries|in the 21st century|\n|Parts of the day|in the morning|\n|Parts of the day|in the afternoon|\n|Parts of the day|in the evening|\n\nExamples:\n\n- My birthday is in July.\n    \n- I was born in 2010.\n    \n- We go swimming in summer.\n    \n- I study English in the morning.\n    \n- My parents watch TV in the evening.\n    \n\nExtra meaning: In can also mean after a period of time.\n\n\"The lesson starts in ten minutes.\"\n\nThis means it starts ten minutes from now."},
		{"id":"66adad179bc635f4","x":627,"y":1931,"width":420,"height":886,"color":"3","type":"text","text":"## Preposition of time: ON\n\nWe use ON for specific days and dates.\n\n## ON = days and dates\n\n|Use|Example|\n|---|---|\n|Days|on Monday|\n|Dates|on 15 March|\n|Special days|on my birthday|\n|Day + part of day|on Friday morning|\n|Specific day|on Christmas Day|\n\nExamples:\n\n- I have English on Tuesday.\n    \n- The exam is on Friday.\n    \n- My birthday is on 12 June.\n    \n- We play football on Saturday afternoon.\n    \n- I visited my grandparents on Christmas Day.\n    \n\nRemember: If we mention a specific day, we normally use ON."},
		{"id":"08f593c283e807f9","x":1220,"y":1931,"width":466,"height":994,"color":"3","type":"text","text":"## Preposition of time: AT\n\nWe use AT for exact times and certain expressions.\n\n## AT = exact times and points in time\n\n| Use             | Example                          |\n| --------------- | -------------------------------- |\n| Clock times     | at 8:30                          |\n| Exact times     | at midnight                      |\n| Exact times     | at noon                          |\n| Expressions     | at night                         |\n| Expressions     | at the weekend (British English) |\n| Holiday periods | at Christmas                     |\n\nExamples:\n\n- School starts at 8:30.\n    \n- I go to bed at 10 o'clock.\n    \n- We sometimes study at night.\n    \n- I play football at the weekend.\n    \n- We visit family at Christmas.\n    \n\nNote: American English often uses on the weekend."},
		{"id":"97c2f238a13de45b","x":1779,"y":1931,"width":506,"height":1542,"color":"3","type":"text","text":"## Other important prepositions of time\n\n### BEFORE and AFTER\n\nBefore means earlier than something.\n\n\"I eat breakfast before school.\"\n\nAfter means later than something.\n\n\"I do my homework after dinner.\"\n\n### DURING\n\nMeans something happens within a particular period or event.\n\n\"I took notes during the lesson.\"\n\n### FOR\n\nTells us how long something lasts.\n\n\"I studied for two hours.\"\n\n### SINCE\n\nTells us when a period started and continues from.\n\n\"I have lived here since 2020.\"\n\nCompare:\n\n- For five years (length of time)\n    \n- Since 2020 (starting point)\n    \n\n### UNTIL\n\nMeans something continues up to a particular time.\n\n\"I studied until 9 o'clock.\"\n\n### BY\n\nMeans something must happen no later than a particular time.\n\n\"Finish your homework by Friday.\"\n\nCompare:\n\n- I worked until 5 o'clock. (I continued working up to 5.)\n    \n- I had finished my work by 5 o'clock. (My work was already complete at 5.)"},
		{"id":"f2369e170ca4c259","x":2430,"y":1931,"width":612,"height":2962,"color":"3","type":"text","text":"# PREPOSITIONS OF PLACE\n\n## 1. IN, ON and AT for location\n\nWe use prepositions of place to describe where a person, animal or object is.\n\n## 2. IN = inside something\n\nWe use IN when something is inside an area, room, building, town, city or country.\n\nExamples:\n\n- The books are in my bag.\n    \n- The students are in the classroom.\n    \n- My phone is in my pocket.\n    \n- She lives in Amsterdam.\n    \n- They live in the Netherlands.\n    \n- There is milk in the fridge.\n    \n\nThink: IN = inside or within an area.\n\n## 3. ON = on a surface\n\nWe use ON when something is touching or resting on a surface.\n\nExamples:\n\n- The book is on the table.\n    \n- The picture is on the wall.\n    \n- My phone is on my desk.\n    \n- The cat is sleeping on the sofa.\n    \n- The keys are on the shelf.\n    \n- There is a poster on the door.\n    \n\nThink: ON = touching a surface.\n\nOther uses:\n\n- The café is on King Street.\n    \n- She is on the bus.\n    \n- The information is on the website.\n    \n\n## 4. AT = a specific point or location\n\nWe use AT when we talk about a particular point, meeting place, destination or activity location.\n\nExamples:\n\n- I am at school.\n    \n- We are at the bus stop.\n    \n- My friend is at the door.\n    \n- She is at home.\n    \n- They are at the cinema.\n    \n- My dad is at work.\n    \n- We met at the station.\n    \n\nThink: AT = a particular point or place.\n\n### IN vs. AT\n\n\"I am at school.\"\n\nThis means school is my location.\n\n\"I am in the classroom.\"\n\nThis means I am inside the classroom.\n\nBoth can be correct, but they focus on different things.\n\n### ON vs. IN for transport\n\n- I am on the bus.\n    \n- She is on the train.\n    \n- We are on the plane.\n    \n- He is in the car.\n    \n- They are in a taxi."},
		{"id":"56acd9dfdfc9d1e5","x":3153,"y":1931,"width":350,"height":266,"color":"3","type":"text","text":"![](data:image/svg+xml;charset=utf-8,%3Csvg%20font-family%3D%22-apple-system-body%2C%20ui-sans-serif%2C%20-apple-system%2C%20system-ui%2C%20%26quot%3BSegoe%20UI%26quot%3B%2C%20Helvetica%2C%20%26quot%3BApple%20Color%20Emoji%26quot%3B%2C%20Arial%2C%20sans-serif%2C%20%26quot%3BSegoe%20UI%20Emoji%26quot%3B%2C%20%26quot%3BSegoe%20UI%20Symbol%26quot%3B%22%20font-weight%3D%22400%22%20data-d-component%3D%22svg%22%20fill%3D%22currentColor%22%20style%3D%22color%3Argb\\(237%2C%20237%2C%20237\\)%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20340%20250%22%20width%3D%22100%25%22%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%2265%22%20y%3D%2223%22%20font-size%3D%2211%22%20font-weight%3D%22600%22%20fill%3D%22currentColor%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%3EIN%3C%2Ftext%3E%3Crect%20x%3D%2231%22%20y%3D%2246%22%20width%3D%2248%22%20height%3D%2243%22%20rx%3D%223%22%20fill%3D%22none%22%20stroke%3D%22%238a93a2%22%20stroke-width%3D%222%22%2F%3E%3Ccircle%20cx%3D%2255%22%20cy%3D%2268%22%20r%3D%229%22%20fill%3D%22%23238b83%22%2F%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%22179%22%20y%3D%2223%22%20font-size%3D%2211%22%20font-weight%3D%22600%22%20fill%3D%22currentColor%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%3EON%3C%2Ftext%3E%3Crect%20x%3D%22145%22%20y%3D%2273%22%20width%3D%2248%22%20height%3D%2213%22%20rx%3D%223%22%20fill%3D%22%238a93a2%22%2F%3E%3Ccircle%20cx%3D%22169%22%20cy%3D%2263%22%20r%3D%2210%22%20fill%3D%22%23238b83%22%2F%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%22293%22%20y%3D%2223%22%20font-size%3D%2211%22%20font-weight%3D%22600%22%20fill%3D%22currentColor%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%3EUNDER%3C%2Ftext%3E%3Crect%20x%3D%22259%22%20y%3D%2245%22%20width%3D%2248%22%20height%3D%2211%22%20rx%3D%223%22%20fill%3D%22%238a93a2%22%2F%3E%3Ccircle%20cx%3D%22283%22%20cy%3D%2276%22%20r%3D%2210%22%20fill%3D%22%23238b83%22%2F%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%2265%22%20y%3D%22148%22%20font-size%3D%2211%22%20font-weight%3D%22600%22%20fill%3D%22currentColor%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%3ENEXT%20TO%3C%2Ftext%3E%3Crect%20x%3D%2229%22%20y%3D%22183%22%20width%3D%2225%22%20height%3D%2226%22%20rx%3D%223%22%20fill%3D%22%238a93a2%22%2F%3E%3Ccircle%20cx%3D%2272%22%20cy%3D%22196%22%20r%3D%2210%22%20fill%3D%22%23238b83%22%2F%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%22179%22%20y%3D%22148%22%20font-size%3D%2211%22%20font-weight%3D%22600%22%20fill%3D%22currentColor%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%3EBETWEEN%3C%2Ftext%3E%3Crect%20x%3D%22140%22%20y%3D%22183%22%20width%3D%2218%22%20height%3D%2227%22%20rx%3D%223%22%20fill%3D%22%238a93a2%22%2F%3E%3Crect%20x%3D%22181%22%20y%3D%22183%22%20width%3D%2218%22%20height%3D%2227%22%20rx%3D%223%22%20fill%3D%22%238a93a2%22%2F%3E%3Ccircle%20cx%3D%22169%22%20cy%3D%22196%22%20r%3D%229%22%20fill%3D%22%23238b83%22%2F%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%22293%22%20y%3D%22148%22%20font-size%3D%2211%22%20font-weight%3D%22600%22%20fill%3D%22currentColor%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%3EABOVE%3C%2Ftext%3E%3Crect%20x%3D%22259%22%20y%3D%22201%22%20width%3D%2248%22%20height%3D%2212%22%20rx%3D%223%22%20fill%3D%22%238a93a2%22%2F%3E%3Ccircle%20cx%3D%22283%22%20cy%3D%22176%22%20r%3D%2210%22%20fill%3D%22%23238b83%22%2F%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E)"},
		{"id":"3ce783e2765d54ab","x":531,"y":3023,"width":454,"height":791,"color":"3","type":"text","text":"\n## Prepositions of time\n\nFill in the gaps with IN, ON or AT.\n\n1. I wake up ___ 7 o'clock.\n    \n2. My birthday is ___ August.\n    \n3. We have English ___ Monday.\n    \n4. I was born ___ 2011.\n    \n5. The film starts ___ 8:30.\n    \n6. We go skiing ___ winter.\n    \n7. My exam is ___ Friday morning.\n    \n8. I study ___ the evening.\n    \n9. My parents sometimes work ___ night.\n    \n10. We visit our grandparents ___ Christmas Day.\n    \n11. I have football practice ___ Thursday.\n    \n12. Our holiday is ___ July."},
		{"id":"26f74dd7c12f9fa0","x":1631,"y":3643,"width":370,"height":754,"type":"text","text":"## Prepositions of place\n\nFill in the gaps with IN, ON or AT.\n\n1. My books are ___ my schoolbag.\n    \n2. The clock is ___ the wall.\n    \n3. We are waiting ___ the bus stop.\n    \n4. She lives ___ Utrecht.\n    \n5. My phone is ___ the table.\n    \n6. He is ___ home.\n    \n7. The students are ___ the classroom.\n    \n8. There is some juice ___ the fridge.\n    \n9. My sister is ___ the train.\n    \n10. My dad is ___ work.\n    \n11. The picture is ___ the wall.\n    \n12. We are meeting ___ the station."}
	],
